<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 20px; color: white; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Vessels Are Big Because... – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Vessels Are Big Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Narrate a short story or incident that is happening now. interactive: Respond to a large variety of classroom commands and instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate, using simple language, a short story about a current event related to the Panama Canal (e.g., 'There are many people at the Canal today. They are watching the vessels pass by.'). Can respond to classroom commands and instructions about the Panama Canal to ask questions and clarify information (e.g., 'Point to the biggest vessel in the picture.' 'The red one?'). Can initiate and sustain conversations on familiar topics about the Canal in collaboration with peers (e.g., 'Which vessel is the biggest: the blue one or the white one?').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of the Panama Canal with several vessels (ships, boats, yachts) passing through. Teacher asks: "What do you see?" "Where is this?" Students respond with familiar words (canal, ship, boat). Teacher elicits the theme: "The vessels are big!" and asks, "What are the vessels doing?" to activate present continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to different vessels in the picture and says, "This vessel is big." "That ship is bigger than the yacht." "The blue vessel is the biggest." "It is traveling through the locks." The teacher slowly models the pronunciation of /k/ in 'Canal' and /b/ in 'boat' and 'big', having students repeat chorally and individually. Teacher also models asking questions: "Is the red vessel big?" "What is the green vessel doing?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a large ship and a small yacht: "Is this ship small or big?" (Expected answer: "Big!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a vessel moving: "Is the vessel moving or still?" (Expected answer: "Moving!" or "It is traveling!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A series of large, numbered flashcards or projected images, each depicting a different scene at the Panama Canal with simple, corresponding sentences. For example: <br>1. Picture of many people watching: "Many people are visiting the Panama Canal today." <br>2. Picture of a large cargo ship: "A big vessel is crossing the canal." <br>3. Picture of two vessels, one bigger: "The black ship is bigger than the red boat." <br>4. Picture of the locks: "The locks connect the oceans." <br>5. Picture of people observing: "They are observing the goods inside the ship."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing current events, comparing things, and describing facts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Teacher shows each flashcard/image and reads the sentence aloud. Students listen. Then, the teacher asks simple gist questions, encouraging short oral answers:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is happening in picture 1?" (Expected: "People are visiting.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which ship is bigger in picture 3?" (Expected: "The black ship.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does the canal connect?" (Expected: "Two oceans.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using Present Simple and Present Continuous for facts and current events, and Comparatives/Superlatives to describe vessels. Incorporating vocabulary: canal, boat, ocean, vessel, ship, yacht, locks, travel, connect, observe, see, visit, big, small, important, historic. Practicing /k/ and /b/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences related to the Panama Canal with visual cues (e.g., a picture of two ships). Students complete the sentences orally. <br>Examples: <br> "The Panama Canal ________ two oceans." (connects) <br> "A big cargo ship ________ through the locks now." (is traveling) <br> "This yacht is _______ (small), but that boat is _______ (smaller)." <br> "That vessel is the _______ (biggest) of all."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames and picture cards (e.g., different types/sizes of vessels, people observing). One student says a sentence, the partner substitutes a word based on a new picture. <br>Example: <br>Student A: "The blue vessel is big." (shows picture of blue vessel) <br>Student B: "The red vessel is bigger." (shows picture of red vessel) <br>Student A: "They are watching the ships." (shows picture of people watching) <br>Student B: "They are observing the water current." (shows picture of water current) <br>Teacher monitors for pronunciation of /k/ and /b/ and correct use of present simple/continuous and comparatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction - "Describe and Compare":</b> Each pair receives a card with an image of a scene at the Panama Canal (e.g., two vessels of different sizes, people doing different actions). Students take turns describing what is happening and comparing elements in their picture, using full sentences. <br>Example: "There are two ships. The white ship is bigger than the blue one. People are watching the ships pass by." "The Panama Canal is important."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair Conversation / Guided Role-play ("Visitors at the Canal").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair pretends they are visiting the Panama Canal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They receive a "Canal Observation Sheet" with 3-4 specific images (e.g., a cargo ship, a cruise ship, people watching, the locks).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns asking and answering questions about what they see in the pictures, narrating events, comparing vessels, and sharing simple opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides guiding prompts on the board: <br> "What is happening now?" <br> "What do you see?" <br> "Is the blue vessel bigger than the red one?" <br> "What do you think about the canal?" (Sociolinguistic: inviting participation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must use present continuous, present simple, and comparatives/superlatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example conversation: <br> Student A: "Look! Many people are watching the canal." <br> Student B: "Yes, they are observing a big vessel. It is crossing the locks." <br> Student A: "Which vessel is bigger, the cargo ship or the cruise ship?" <br> Student B: "The cargo ship is bigger. It's the biggest one here!" <br> Student A: "I like the canal because it is historic. What do you think?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the pair conversation (Performance stage).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Visitors at the Canal" pair conversation, narrating current events, comparing vessels, and responding to questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear sounds /k/ and /b/, words are understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses words like 'vessel,' 'ship,' 'locks,' 'travel,' 'observe,' 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Uses Present Simple and Present Continuous correctly in affirmative and interrogative forms. Correctly uses comparatives and superlatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fluency and Interaction:</b> Can initiate and sustain a short conversation with a peer, responding to questions and inviting participation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Task Completion:</b> Successfully narrates short events and compares vessels based on the given images.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete these sentences orally or by pointing to a thumbs up/down: <br>"Today I can talk about what is happening at the Panama Canal." <br>"I can say which vessel is bigger."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you when talking about the Panama Canal today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when comparing the vessels with your partner?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this speaking lesson? The visual aids and guided prompts helped students engage in conversation. The pair work allowed for more individual speaking turns. What adjustments are needed? Some students still struggle with distinguishing between Present Simple (facts) and Present Continuous (current events); more explicit practice or color-coding might help. The pronunciation of 'through' was challenging for some. For next lesson, I will ensure specific scaffolding for writing these comparisons and narrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use the sentences and expressions from today's speaking activities to write short descriptions about the Panama Canal, focusing on current events and comparisons, preparing them to articulate their observations in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>